Therefore I was thrilled to see a post on sci.physics.electromag (a new
group, to me anyway) discussing nano-tesla magnetic sensors. Potentially
cheap ones too! Magnetic sensors have the potential to pinpoint more
specific neuro-responces (with differential techniques) and paint a more
detailed picture of what's going on in there (in your brain). 

Has anyone on the list done any work in this area?

Here's the post:

============================ begin clipped post ==============================

Researching the literature, I recently found a new magnetic field sensor 
based on the Faraday effect in thick epitaxial films of magnetic garnets 
of the type used in magneto-optic isolators.

Anyone know of a source for single crystals of substituted yttrium iron
garnet (YIG)?  Would really like to get my hands on some of these and make
a sensor!  

Preliminary research by Bell Labs indicates that such devices are capable
of very high frequency response (in the GHz) and very high sensitivity 
(like 1 nT ).  These qualities are a trade-off since maximum sensitivity
requires low magnetic moment and thicker crystals, which conflicts with
requirements for high speed. 

Here's how it works:  The growth of magnetic domains parallel to the
applied field results in changing Faraday rotation of the plane of
polarization of transmitted infrared light.  For thick films, the
magnetization increases linearly with field and when it saturates, all of
the stripe domains are wiped out. 

To make a sensor, you use linearly polarized IR light to probe a large
number of stripe domains in the YIG crystal (or thin film).  Then you
measure the light with an analyzer set at 45 degrees to the input
polarization.  The sensitivity is inversely proportional to the
magnetization and linearly proportional to the film thickness times the
Faraday rotation constant. 

If you are able to do this, you get a very cool little sensor that does
not produce a magnetic field of its own, like a fluxgate or
magnetoresistor.  These sensors also have very low power requirements,
unlike proton precession magnetometers and SQUIDs.  For our purposes,
which include implantation in biological entities, these is very desirable
qualities. 

{ Don't worry, you're not a candidate; we're only putting these in fish }

I'm working under a National Science Foundation grant to develop sensors
for tracking the global movement of fishes.  We intend to use the
non-dipole component of the geomagnetic field to obtain latitude
information from implanted microcontrollers.  If we can do this, it will
be far more accurate than any  method now in use.  Since there is a
very good chance that the fish use geomagnetic fields for their own
navigation, we don't want to confuse them by generating a field. 


Hope some of you have an interest in electromagnetic sensors.  I would 
enjoy sharing information and experimental findings with you...


