>...I was not sure whether any natural rights were supposed to have preceded
>homo sapiens, but you later point out that there are no 'metaphysical
>things external to people called "rights," ' and together with your two-
>sentence proof from a few months ago on why beasts can't have rights
>(which I like), this clearly answers my question about pre-human existence.

Good sound-bite, eh?  I once got the chance to use it when I was interviewed
for counter-demonstrating against animal rights protesters by a local TV
station.  Apparently it went over well.

>You mention "negative" rights so does this exclude the right to a free
>data super-highway?

Nope.  That would be a positive claim right.  As the Rasmussen and Den Uyl
point out in "Liberty and Nature," the two characteristics of natural rights
are negativity and universality.  Negativity means the right not to be
interfered with, not the right to force others to provide you with any goods
or services.  Universality means it applies to everybody.

>...how about the right not to have your messages tampered
>with on the data super-highway, or the right not to have your neighbour's
>AI nanotech slaves run riot and invade your home?

Definitely yes to the latter.  If, by the former, you mean the right not to
have your communications interfered with without your permission, then yes
to that one as well.  That's easily derived from the right to freedom of
speech/expression, and the other one from the right to property.

>>I prefer the terminology of the Austrian school of economics, that s.o.'s
>>are the results of human action but not human design.
>
>That would describe the "man-made" s.o.'s such as money and memetic
>evolution, but aren't there also natural s.o.'s such as biological
>evolution and self-regulation of ecosystems?

Good point.  This calls for further terminological revision.  I'll have to
sleep on it.

>If rights were never enforced or respected there would be no evidence for
>existence or pre-existence;

Why not?  What does enforcement have to do with the evidence for them?

>Since rights can be spontaneous orders I now think it's
>better to speak of "creation" rather than "construction".  Construction
>implies design, creation may be with or without intent. 

I'm afraid "creation" really does entail intent.  Hayek suggests "forma-
tion," but I'm not quite satisfied with it, either.

>The theory of rights seems to have an inherent inaccuracy, though, since
>there is much uncertainty regarding enforcement and recognition.  Hardly
>anyone even thinks about enforcing their right to non-coercion or right
>to life, and statists certainly don't respect these.

This is an issue of abstraction.  Since most people don't think abstractly
about these things, but concretely, they think of concrete rights.  But
those who do think about these things abstractly, such as law professors,
often talk of such abstract rights as the right to individual autonomy,
self-expression, the right to be left alone, etc.

>  If the theory
>implies that rights will increasingly be enforced as they're discovered,
>I hope it turns out to be true. 

Sort of.  The discoveries have to be acted upon, too, and that's not always
possible.  For instance, natural rights were known to the Scholastics during
the Crusades in great clarity and detail, but little use was made of this
knowledge until the Dutch broke free of the Spanish Empire - proceeding to
become one of the most prosperous societies in the world upon the enforcement
of natural rights as they understood them.

>There seems to be uncertainty in the theory whether you have it that all
>possible rights exist but they're not uniformly enforced, or that opinions
>vary as to which rights are objective and which are fictions. 

Both.  Unfortunately, since the statists found that they were unable to
persuade the people to give up the rights they derided as "nonsense on
stilts," as Bentham protested the French Declaration of the Rights of
Man, they turned instead to framing their proposals in terms of such
rights as the right to security, medicine, a job, etc.

So, we're caught in the curious position in which one camp insists that
negative rights are fictions, as opposed to positive ones which are
objective, and another camp holds precisely the opposite.  This
compounds the usual difficulty in the discovery and enforcement of rights.

