From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Fri, 4 Mar 1994 02:15:25 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-56] Optimal Statism?

I've been wondering lately: is there an optimal amount of statism?

I don't mean to imply that any statism can be good, only that it may
be possible that it can cost too much to get rid of some than would
be affordable.  Of course, this all depends upon the circumstances
determining such affordability, but I'll get to that in a moment.

First, think of the cost of defending yourself against any free lance
criminal.  Clearly, it would be silly to spend $150 to defend against
$100 loss, all other things being equal.  So, what if it costs $150
to defend against $100 in losses from a particular statist measure?

This leads to a sort of a minimal state, but from another direction
besides the notion that there's any moral justification for it.
Rather, that it's possible that we'll have to learn to live with
the enemy, holding him at bay maximally, but unable to completely
get rid of him.

What determines how much statism we can afford to get rid of?  The
answer seems to be prosperity, which is caused by the efficient use
of production techniques.  If this is correct, then economic growth
would imply that we could afford to get rid of more statism, and
quantum leaps in prosperity would make quantum reductions in statism
affordable - and, unfortunately, the contrary would also be true.
Poverty would make statism harder to resist.

Now, what if the Information Revolution causes such a burst of
prosperity?  I guess I'm sort of talking myself into the sort of
optimism Duncan Frissell espouses.  I'm reminded of something ISIL's
President, Vince Miller, was told by his czech guide when visiting
Prague.  Vince commented about how ugly the Soviet apartment blocks
were, and asked why they weren't being torn down.  The reply was:
"We don't need to tear them down.  They'll fall down by themselves."
I'm beginning to think this is the proper attitude towards the
American Empire.  It's in terminal decline, and there's little it
can do about it.  And we don't have to tear it down, it'll fall all
by itself.  All we have to do is build our own alternative social
structures.

But this may not result in a totally anarchist world - not because
of any flaw in anarco-capitalism, but simply because we may reach
limits to how much we can "limit" the State, and have to content
ourselves with a substantially freer world than we have now, and
hope for more progress in the future.

