Subject: [#94-3-60] ANTHRO: Multiple origins of modern homo-sapiens

Simon!:

> As I understood the article, the recent discovery suggested that homo
> *sapiens* may have arisen independently in different locations from
> groups of homo erectus that left Africa.  But the details were
> sketchy, and I don't recall the article event mentioning homo erectus
> by name.

I think I've found the cause of the confusion.  There have been two
recent advanced in palaeoanthropology, reported in Nature, Science and
New Scientist, which we're mixing together.

First, the Swisher find is of fragments from 3 homo-*erectuses* in Java
1.8-1.6? MYears.  This has no bearing on the homo-*sapiens*
multiregional debate.  The Java finds indicate that, most likely,
erectus spread out from Africa much earlier and more quickly than
previously thought - no habilis remains have been found outside Africa. 
The antiquity of the Java discovery indicates that the early expansion
of erectus predates the Acheulean tool phase of handaxes and bifacial
flints.  East Asian erectus tools' remained less complex than
European/W.Asian erectus - something that was hard to explain before
since you'd expect the more advanced Acheulean tool culture to move east
with the migration of erectus.  Now it seems that erectus colonised Asia
before the Acheulean phase, end of mystery.  Now it seems that the
Acheulean development is unique to western erectus, after the split into
possibly a western and eastern sub species.

Second, a homo-*sapiens* skull in China, found 10 years ago that was
claimed to have local ethnic characteristics, has been convincingly
dated, by a number of ways, to approx 200,000 years, which is not long
after (eastern?) erectus died out.  The claim is that this strengthens
the link between local erectus and sapiens - thus boosting the
multiregional hypothesis (which is that homo-sapiens evolved from homo-
erectus separately in different regions of the world at approx the same
time).  The more I think about the multiregional hypothesis the less
likely it seems.  Species replacement does not normally (ever?  Care to
comment, Jay?) proceed in parallel.  Also, I understand, it is in
conflict with genetic studies (Sci Am had a couple of articles on this
a while back), although there is still the possibility of a mixture of
the two hypotheses.  Homo-sapiens may evolved once and then spread out
absorbing some of the erectus gene pool.

BTW, Simon!, that was interesting stuff about language.  Perhaps you
could comment on something which puzzles me.  The genetic race map of
homo-sapiens (us!) lineages seem similar to the super-family tree of
languages.  But the diversification timescales are different by an order
of magnitude - 100,000s versus 10,000s years antiquity.  It's early days
in both depratments, I know, but am I seeing imaginary patterns or could
the old languages have evolved much more slowly than we presently think?

